# Synergistic Regulation of Polysulfides Conversion and Deposition by MOF‐Derived Hierarchically Ordered Carbonaceous Composite for High‐Energy Lithium–Sulfur Batteries

## abstract
To achieve a high sulfur loading is critical for high‐energy lithium–sulfur batteries. However, high sulfur loading, especially at a low electrolyte/sulfur ratio (E/S), usually causes low sulfur utilization, mainly caused by the slow redox kinetics of polysulfides and the passivation of the discharge product, poor electrically/ionically conducting Li2S. Herein, by using cobalt‐based metal organic frameworks (Co‐MOFs) as precursors, a Co, N‐doped carbonaceous composite (Co, N‐CNTs (carbon nanotubes)‐CNS (carbon nanosheet)/CFC (carbon fiber cloth)) is fabricated with hierarchically ordered structure, which consists of a free‐standing 3D carbon fiber skeleton decorated with a vertical 2D carbon nanosheets array rooted by interwoven 1D CNTs. As an effective polysulfides host, the hierarchically ordered 3D conductive network with abundant active sites and voids can effectively trap polysulfides and provide fast electron/ions pathways to convert them. In addition, Co and N heteroatoms can strengthen the interaction with polysulfides and accelerate its reaction kinetics. More importantly, the interwoven CNTs with Co, N‐doping can induce 3D Li2S deposition instead of conventional 2D deposition, which benefits improving sulfur utilization. Therefore, for Co, N‐CNTs‐CNS/CFC electrodes, even at a high sulfur loading of 10.20 mg cm−2 with a low E/S of 6.94, a high reversible areal capacity of 7.42 mAh cm−2 can be achieved with excellent cycling stability.

To achieve a high sulfur loading is critical for high-energy lithium–sulfur batteries. However, high sulfur loading, especially at a low electrolyte/sulfur ratio (E/S), usually causes low sulfur utilization, mainly caused by the slow redox kinetics of polysulfides and the passivation of the discharge product, poor electrically/ionically conducting $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ . Herein, by using cobalt-based metal organic frameworks (Co-MOFs) as precursors, a Co, N-doped carbonaceous composite (Co, N-CNTs (carbon nanotubes)-CNS (carbon nanosheet)/CFC (carbon fiber cloth)) is fabricated with hierarchically ordered structure, which consists of a free-standing 3D carbon fiber skeleton decorated with a vertical 2D carbon nanosheets array rooted by interwoven 1D CNTs. As an effective polysulfides host, the hierarchically ordered 3D conductive network with abundant active sites and voids can effectively trap polysulfides and provide fast electron/ions pathways to convert them. In addition, Co and N heteroatoms can strengthen the interaction with polysulfides and accelerate its reaction kinetics. More importantly, the interwoven CNTs with Co, N-doping can induce 3D $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ deposition instead of conventional 2D deposition, which benefits improving sulfur utilization. Therefore, for Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrodes, even at a high sulfur loading of $10.20~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with a low $E/S$ of 6.94, a high reversible areal capacity of $7.42~\mathsf{m A h}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ can be achieved with excellent cycling stability.
## 1. Introduction
Lithium–sulfur batteries (LSBs) have been treated as a promising candidate for nextgeneration energy storage devices for its high theoretical capacity $(1675\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}})$ , low cost, and environmentally friendliness.[1] However, the conductivity of sulfur (S) and its reduction produce $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S})$ is very poor, which limits the utilization of active material. In addition, the shuttle effect of dissolved long-chain polysulfides in electrolyte would result in low coulombic efficiency and poor cycling stability.[2]
To tackle the above problems, many efforts have been devoted to design various kinds of conductive nanomaterials as effective sulfur hosts, such as porous carbon, graphene/graphene oxide, carbon nanotubes (CNTs), metal oxide/sulfide, and conductive polymers.[3] Although the cycling stability and rate performance of Li–S batteries have been significantly improved, the sulfur loading in these cathode materials is usually very low (less than $2.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ . In addition, to fabricate sulfur cathode, these nanomaterials also need conductive agents, polymer binders, and metal current collectors, which would further decrease the energy density. Low sulfur loading and low energy density make these sulfur cathode materials far from the practical demands for lithium– sulfur batteries.
In order to meet the practical demands, free-standing 3D carbonaceous current collectors have been popularly used to fabricate high-energy sulfur electrode, such as carbon fiber cloth (CFC), graphene foam, and cotton carbon.[4] However, due to the limited surface area and weak interaction with polysulfides, these 3D carbonaceous current collectors are usually needed to incorporate with other functional nanomaterials, such as N-doped CNTs,[5] graphene,[6] metal (Co)/metal compounds $(\mathrm{TiO}_{2},~\mathrm{WS}_{2},~\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}),{\mathrm{[7]}}$ or their hybrids.[8] These nanomaterials can not only provide more active sites but also strengthen the interaction between polysulfides and sulfur cathodes and promote redox reaction, thus improving the electrochemical properties. Although these 3D sulfur cathodes can increase the sulfur loading significantly, the high sulfur loading is usually counterbalanced by low sulfur utilization, especially at a low E/S.[9] Therefore, to achieve high sulfur utilization at a high sulfur loading with a low E/S is still a big challenge.
Low sulfur utilization is mainly caused by the following two problems: the slow redox kinetics of polysulfides and the passivation of poor electrically/ionically conducting $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ . Most previous works on design of 3D sulfur cathodes mainly focused on solving the former but ignored the latter. While, the structure and morphology of solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ significantly influences the sulfur utilization.[10] Several previous works have proved that the structure and morphology of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ can be controlled by modifying carbon surface with functional groups, manipulating the electrolyte solvent properties, adding an organic redox mediator, or employing electrochemical strategy.[10a,11] However, for highenergy sulfur cathode design, there is still little knowledge about how to control the morphology of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ through rational structure and composition design, not to mention to design a 3D sulfur cathode that can handle the above two problems simultaneously.
Among different kinds of 3D carbon hosts, the CFC was a promising binder-free substrate as sulfur cathode due to its high mechanical strength and excellent electrical conductivity.[12] The doping of N atoms has been demonstrated to effectively strengthen the interaction between polysulfides and carbonbased cathodes.[13] In addition, Co atoms (as sulfide compound) not only show the strong chemisorption to polysulfides but also can promote their conversion.[14] Inspired by the above discoveries, by using cobalt-based metal organic frameworks (Co-MOFs) as precursor, we developed a simple two-step method to fabricate interwoven 1D CNTs rooted on 2D carbon nanosheets (CNSs) array with Co and N doping, which themselves vertically grew on 3D CFC (denoted as Co, N-CNTs-CNS/ CFC) as shown in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information. Benefiting from the multiscale, hierarchically ordered integrate conductive network with abundant pores, Co, N-CNTs-CNS/ CFC can provide large amount of active sites and fast electron/ ions pathways to anchor and convert polysulfides. The codoping of Co and N heteroatoms can perform a synergistic effect on immobilizing polysulfides and accelerate their reaction kinetics, which is also confirmed by the theoretical calculations. More importantly, the interwoven CNTs with Co, N-doping can change the morphology of solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ nucleation on CNSs from conventional 2D to 3D porous structure (Figure  1), which further induces the following 3D growth of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , leading to improved sulfur utilization. Therefore, as a polysulfides host, Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC exhibits excellent electrochemical properties in terms of long-term cycling stability and superior rate performance. Even at a high sulfur loading of $10.20~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ with a low E/S value of 6.94, a high reversible areal capacities of $7.42\mathrm{~mAh~cm}^{-2}$ can be achieved, which is remarkable among recently related works. We believe this work could provide an inspiring insight for high-energy sulfur cathode design.
## 2. Result and Discussion
Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC was fabricated by a simple two-step process as shown in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information. First, a piece of CFC was immersed in a beaker containing 2-methylimidazole (2-MIM) and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in deionized water for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ and then the color of the CFC turned from black to blue (Figure S2, Supporting Information). Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images in Figure S3 in the Supporting Information show that the carbon fibers of the CFC were successfully covered by Co-MOFs nanosheets array and the product Co-MOFs array/CFC was obtained. Second, the CoMOFs array/CFC was turned into Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC by a further pyrolysis in $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ (1:9 by volume) with N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) vapor as an extra carbon source.
The structure and composition of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC were investigated by SEM and transmission electron microscope (TEM). As shown in Figure  2a, after pyrolysis, the Co-MOFs array turns into CNSs array, which retains the original structure well. The amplified SEM images (Figure 2b,c) clearly exhibit that these CNSs are rooted by many interwoven CNTs with abundant mesopores. Many Co nanoparticles, marked by red circles, are widely dispersed in the interwoven CNTs in Figure  2c. In Figure  2d, the TEM image of a CNS, a large number of black nanodots (assigned to Co nanoparticles) are uniformly distributed on the CNS. The further amplified TEM image in Figure  2e shows some Co nanoparticles with a size around $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ are encapsulated by the CNTs, which is further confirmed by the high-resolution TEM of one tip of the CNTs in Figure  2f. The lattice distances of 0.21 and $0.36\ \mathrm{nm}$ correspond to Co (111) and C (002), respectively, demonstrating the catalysis of Co nanoparticles in the growth of the CNTs.[15] The element mapping in Figure  2g further demonstrates the uniform distribution of Co and N on the CNS. The codoping of Co and N is beneficial for trapping and converting polysulfides.[16]
In order to investigate the role of DMF vapor during pyrolysis, we also calcinated Co-MOFs array/CFC under the same condition without DMF vapor. In this case, the Co-MOFs array changed into Co, N-doped CNSs array with few CNTs on the surface (denoted as Co, N-CNS/CFC) as shown in Figure S4a,b in the Supporting Information. From the magnified SEM images in Figure S4c,d in the Supporting Information, many Co nanoparticles with size around $20\mathrm{nm}$ are well dispersed on the surface of on CNS in Co, N-CNS/CFC. Without DMF as extra carbon source, these Co nanoparticles cannot catalyze the growth of CNTs. Such a contrast between Co, N-CNS/CFC and Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC demonstrates that DMF vapor should provide carbon source for the growth of CNTs.
Crystal phase of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC, Co, N-CNS/CFC, and CFC were investigated by X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements (Figure  3a). Different from the CFC, which possesses two broad peaks at $26^{\circ}$ and $43^{\circ}$ indexed to partially graphitized carbon, Co, N-CNS/CFC, and Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC exhibit three peaks corresponding to metallic cobalt (Joint Committee on Powder Diffraction Standards, 15-0806), confirming the existence of Co nanoparticles in their carbon matrix. The broad Co peaks with low intensities of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC demonstrate the ultrasmall size of Co nanoparticles,[17] which is consistent with the SEM and TEM results. Figure 3b shows Raman spectra of these samples, which all have two typical broad peaks at about 1350 and $1580\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to D and G band of carbon, respectively.[18] The intensity ratio of the D band to G band $\left({I_{\mathrm{D}}}/{I_{\mathrm{G}}}\right)$ is negatively correlated with the degree of carbon graphitization.[19] Hence, a little decrease of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ from CFC to Co, N-CNS/CFC demonstrates the improved graphitization by metal Co during the heating process. The lowest value of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratio of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC should be contributed to the highly graphitized CNTs as observed in the above SEM and TEM results. The highly graphitized CNTs benefits fast electron transportation.
The surface chemistry of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC was examined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The survey spectrum demonstrates the existence of element Co and N in the carbonaceous material (Figure  3c). The relative atomic contents of Co and N in the Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC are around 0.28 and $1.27\ \mathrm{at\%}$ , respectively. Such a low content of Co is contrast to the results from the above SEM and TEM tests. For XPS is only a surface measurement technique, the contrast demonstrates that most Co nanoparticles are encapsulated by the carbon,[20] which can help to avoid side reaction between polysulfides and Co nanoparticles.[1a] The two peaks in the high-resolution $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum, located at 778.4 and $780.0\ \mathrm{eV},$ are assigned to Metallic Co and Co–N (Figure 3d). In addition, the high-resolution XPS spectrum of N 1s can be divided into two peaks, corresponding to Pyridinic N (398.6  eV) and Graphitic N $\textsf{J}(400.9\ \mathrm{eV})$ , as shown in Figure 3e. Moreover, the highresolution C1s spectrum can be fitted into three peaks, where the one located at $285.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ was attributed to the $\mathrm{C-N}$ bond, which further confirms the N doping in the carbon matrix. The XPS survey spectrum of Co, N-CNS/CFC is similar to that of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC, which also possesses Co and N heteroatoms as shown in Figure S5 in the Supporting Information. Considering the above results, Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC should be a promising cathode material for Li–S battery for the following reasons: 1) Vertical CNSs array with interwoven CNTs on CFC can enhance the surface area and provide more active sites for polysulfides conversion. 2) The hierarchical structure with abundant pores can not only accommodate volume change of active material during repeated lithiation/delithiation to keep structure stability but also benefit impregnation of electrolyte and transfer of lithium ions. 3) Co and N heteroatoms can strengthen the interaction between cathode and polysulfides and accelerate the redox kinetics.
To test the application potential of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC for Li–S battery, we assembled batteries by using Co, N-CNTs-CNS/ CFC loaded with certain polysulfides as sulfur cathodes and lithium metal as anodes, then carried a series of electrochemical tests. For contrast, Co, N-CNS/CFC and CFC electrodes were also tested by using the same method. Figure  4a shows the charge–discharge profiles of different electrodes at $0.1~\mathrm{C}$ ( $1\mathrm{~C~}=1675\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}})$ . All the profiles possess two discharge plateaus and two charge plateaus. The higher discharge plateau at around $2.3\mathrm{~V~}$ corresponds to the reduction of $S_{8}$ to form high-ordered polysulfides $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{n}$ , $4<n\leq8$ . While, the lower discharge plateau located at around $2.1\mathrm{~V~}$ contributes to the further conversion of high-ordered polysulfides into insoluble $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ .[21] Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC reveals lower polarization and higher specific capacities than the other two electrodes. As shown Figure  4b, the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves exhibit two pairs of characteristic peaks, which are consistent with the charge–discharge profiles. It should be noted that the CV curve of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC shows the largest enclosed CV area and the sharpest characteristic peaks with the smallest gaps between cathodic and anodic peaks among the three electrodes. In addition, according to the electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) in Figure  4c, Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC also displays the smallest charge-transfer resistance. Furthermore, EIS recorded after 30 cycles shows the similar trend and Co, N-CNTs-CNS/ CFC still obtains the lowest charge-transfer resistance among the three electrodes (Figure S6, Supporting Information). The above results demonstrate the faster electrochemical kinetics and higher utilization of polysulfides in Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC than CFC and Co, N-CNS/CFC, which should be due to that the decoration of CFC with CNSs array and interwoven CNTs can provide extra electron/ions pathways and more active sites to increase the reaction kinetics of polysulfides. The redox kinetics of polysulfides can be also reflected by the rate performances of the three electrodes. As shown in Figure 4d, at different current rates of 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, and $2\mathrm{~C~}$ , Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrode delivered discharge capacities of 1185, 1095, 994, 914, and $779\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, obviously higher than the other electrodes. When the current rate returned to $0.1\mathrm{~C~}$ , the discharge capacity of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrode can still be $1108\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ , demonstrating outstanding reversibility. The excellent rate performance of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrode also confirms that the synergistic effect of CNSs array and CNTs plays a vital role on the improved redox kinetics of polysulfides. Figure  4e shows the long-term cycling performances of the three electrodes at $0.5\mathrm{~C~}$ after three cycles activation at $0.1~\mathrm{C}$ Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrode exhibits a high discharge capacity of $926{\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}}$ at the fourth cycle and maintains the discharge capacity of $766~\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}$ after 250 cycles at $0.5\mathrm{~C~}$ with a high capacity retention of $82.7\%$ , corresponding to a low decay rate of $0.07\%$ per cycle. While, the discharge capacities of Co, N-CNS/CFC and CFC electrodes are much lower. Besides, the average coulombic efficiency of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC during the long-term cycling is $97.0\%$ , which is also higher than that of Co, N-CNS/CFC $(96.8\%)$ and CFC electrodes $(95.0\%)$ .
To understand the reasons behind the excellent cycling stability and the high coulombic efficiency of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/ CFC, we dissembled the batteries with Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC and its counterparts (CFC and Co, N-CNS/CFC) after 100 cycles. Figure S7 in the Supporting Information reveals that Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrode maintained structure well after 100 cycles, demonstrating the outstanding structure stability. Besides, the surface of lithium anode from cycled battery with Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrode was much smoother than those with Co, N-CNS/CFC and CFC electrodes (Figure S8, Supporting Information), indicating few polysulfides passing through separator to react with lithium anode during the long cycling test.[22] The interaction between polysulfides and the three electrodes was examined by the adsorption test in Figure S9 in the Supporting Information. After immersing in 0.01  m $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ solution for $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ , Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrode completely decolored the dark yellow color in the glass bottle. While, Co, N-CNS/CFC only turned the dark yellow color into the light yellow. In contrast, CFC electrode did not change the dark yellow color a little. Thus, the polysulfides adsorption abilities of the three electrodes should be as the following order: Co, N-CNTs-CNS $\mathrm{^{\prime}C F C}>\mathrm{Co}$ , N-CNS/CFC $>$ CFC. XPS was further used to test Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrode after adsorbing $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ solution. The new peak assigned to $\mathrm{~S~}2\mathrm{p}$ is observed in the survey spectrum in Figure S10a in the Supporting Information, indicating $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ adsorbed on Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC. As shown in Figure S10b in the Supporting Information, the high-resolution $\mathrm{~S~}2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum, two peaks at 162.0 and $164.4\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to terminal and bridging sulfur atoms of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ .[23] The peaks at 167.5 and $168.6\mathrm{~eV}$ are ascribed to $S{\mathrm{-}}C0$ bond,[24] indicating the chemical interaction between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ and Co. This is also confirmed by the slight shift of the $\mathrm{Co-N}$ and metallic Co peaks and the appearance of ${\mathrm{Co}}{-}{\mathrm{S}}$ peak after adsorbing $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ in the high-resolution Co $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ spectrum (Figure S10c in the Supporting Information). Figure S10d in the Supporting Information shows that, after adsorbing $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ , the peaks of graphitic $\mathrm{~N~}$ and pyridinic N shift positively due to $\operatorname{Li-N}$ bonding, demonstrating the chemical bonding of $\mathrm{~N~}$ with $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ .[25] Therefore, the strong chemical interaction with polysulfides endows that Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC can effectively inhibit shuttle effect of polysulfides and accelerate the redox kinetics of polysulfides, causing the excellent long-term cycling stability and high coulombic efficiency retention.
In order to further substantiate the effect of Co nanoparticles and doped N heteroatoms on the polysulfides immobilization and conversion, density functional theory (DFT) calculation is performed to investigate the interaction between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and pristine graphene, N-doped graphene and N-doped or pristine graphene-encapsulated Co nanoparticles with (111) surface, which is consistent with the experimental results. Notably, in 1,3-dioxolane $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{(DOL)}}/1,2$ -dimethoxyethane (DME) (1:1 by volume) electrolyte solvent, both of which are low donor-number solvents, the main reaction intermediate of L–S redox reactions is $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ , which determines the redox kinetics of polysulfides and thereby affects the eletrochemical properties of Li–S batteries.[26] Hence, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ is more suitable than $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ as the representative to do DFT calculation in order to make sure that the calculation results can be more reliable. Figure 5a displays the differential charge density of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ adsorbed on the different substrates with optimized configurations, which shows a visualized bonding information with electron accumulation between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and these substrates. The corresponding binding energy $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{E}}_{\mathrm{b}}$ is shown in Figure  5b. Specifically, $\ensuremath{E_{\mathrm{b}}}$ between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ and different substrates is listed as the following order: Graphene $(-0.52~\mathrm{eV})<$ Graphitic N $\{(-0.65~\mathrm{eV})<\mathrm{Co}@ $ Graphene $(-0.89\mathrm{\eV})<\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Gr}$ aphitic N $(-0.93\mathrm{\mathrm{~eV})\leq\mathrm{Pyri.}}$ - dinic N $(-1.46~\mathrm{eV})<\mathrm{Co}\textcircled{\sc}]$ Pyridinic N $(-1.56~\mathrm{eV})$ . The above results confirm that the N-doping can enhance the interaction between graphene and polysulfides and the introduction of Co nanoparticles can further strengthen the favorable effect. The strong interaction also benefits the interfacial charge transfer. Thus, compared to other four substrates, much more electrons are transferred from $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ to Pyridinic $\mathrm{~N~}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}{\ @}{\mathrm{pyri}}.$ - dinic N (Figure  5c), which benefits improving the redox of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ . Figure  5d,e shows the density of states (DOS) for Graphitic N, Pyridinic N, $\mathbf{Co}\textcircled{a}$ pyridinic N, and $\operatorname{Co}(\varnothing)$ graphitic N, respectively, all of which have spreading states around Fermi level with zero bandgap, indicating the metallic properties. This demonstrates that graphene can still maintain satisfactory electric conductivity after the doping of N heteroatoms or the introduction of Co particles, which benefits electronic transportation. Moreover, the DOS of Pyridinic N, $\operatorname{Co}(\varnothing)$ graphitic N, and $\operatorname{Co}(\varnothing)$ pyridinic N possess abundant electrons in the upper states near Fermi level. These electrons are easier to participate in conversion of polysulfides, thus leading to better electrochemical properties. The above calculation results demonstrate the introduction of Co and N heteroatoms into carbonaceous cathodes benefits trapping polysulfides and improving their redox kinetics.
SEM was further carried to investigate the morphologies of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ on the surface of one CNS of Co, N-CNS/CFC and Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC at the three different states of discharge, which includes the initial, the middle, and the end of the low voltage plateau. Figure 6a shows the first-discharge voltage profiles of Co, N-CNS/CFC and Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC, in which the corresponding states for Figure $6\mathsf{b{-g}}$ are marked on the curves. Upon the initial of the low voltage plateau, the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ nucleation starts to form. As shown in Figure  6b, the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ on the CNS of Co, N-CNS/CFC is 2D, thin, and uniform, which totally covers the Co nanoparticles on the surface (Figure S4, Supporting Information). While, the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ on the CNS of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC is 3D porous (Figure  6e), induced by the interwoven CNTs. As the discharge continues to the middle of the low voltage plateau, the 2D $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ film on the CNS of Co, N-CNS/CFC becomes thicker (Figure  6c), which should be due to the typical 2D lateral growth of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ .[27] Whereas, that many 3D $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ particles stack on each other with some pores is observed on the CNS of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC (Figure  6f), which demonstrates the 3D growth of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}.^{[28]}$ By the end of the low voltage plateau, which is also the end of discharge, although the insulating $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ layer on the CNS of Co, N-CNS/ CFC become so thick that the CNS substrate is hard to distinguish (Figure 6d), the surface of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ is still flat and smooth. In contrast, the surface of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ on the CNS of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/ CFC becomes much rougher, since the 3D $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ particles grow larger (Figure  6g). Furthermore, the energy dispersive X-ray spectra of the above discharge products at different discharge states were collected to confirm them as $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ or solid polysulfide species (Table S1, Supporting Information). Based on the above observations, it can be inferred that the interwoven Co, N-doped CNTs change the growth of solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ precipitates on CNSs from conventional 2D to 3D, which enables higher utilization of polysulfides. This is confirmed by the voltage profiles in Figure  6a, in which the discharge capacity of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC is much higher than that of Co, N-CNS/ CFC. Besides, the interwoven CNTs construct an in-built micro3D conductive networks in $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ deposits, promoting electrons transfer. Furthermore, the rougher surface of 3D $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ would obviously increase the interface with electrolyte, benefiting the fast transportation of Li ions. Hence, 3D structure can make $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ be easily and completely reutilized during charge. The 3D growth of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ on Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC should derive from the 3D porous nucleation at the initial $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ nucleation, which could be due to the spatially selective precipitation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ on the Co, N-doped 3D porous CNTs networks. In other words, during the discharge, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ would prefer to deposit on the polar sites of CNTs doped by Co and N heteroatoms rather than the sites without them, which has been confirmed by the above calculation results. Since $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ prefer to deposit on the formed $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ instead of an even deposition[10a] and the 3D interwoven CNTs with abundant mesopores provide enough space for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ deposition, thus, the 3D porous nucleation induces the following 3D growth of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ .
To achieve high sulfur loading is necessary for practical application of Li–S batteries. Thus, the cycling performances of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC at high sulfur loadings of 5.1 and $10.2\ \mathrm{mg\cm}^{-2}$ were also measured. As shown in Figure $\mathrm{\Delta}7\mathrm{a}$ , at the sulfur loading of $5.1\mathrm{\mg\cm^{-2}}$ , Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC exhibits the areal capacity of $4.69\mathrm{\mAh\cm^{-2}}$ at the second cycle and maintains the areal capacity of $4.38\:\mathrm{\mAh\cm^{-2}}$ after 39 cycles at $0.1\mathrm{~C~}$ , which is higher than commercial Li-ion batteries (commonly $4\ \mathrm{mAh\cm^{-2}},$ .[29] At $0.2~\mathrm{C}$ with the same sulfur loading, Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC reveals the areal capacity of $4.51\mathrm{\mAh\cm^{-2}}$ at the second cycle and retains the areal capacity of $3.90\mathrm{\mAh\cm^{-2}}$ after 70 cycles with a high areal capacity retention of $86.5\%$ (Figure S11, Supporting Information). When the sulfur loading was further increased to $10.2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with a low E/S value of 6.94, the areal capacity approaches to $7.42\:\mathrm{\mAh}\:\mathrm{\cm^{-2}}$ after seven cycles at $0.1\mathrm{~C~}$ with excellent cycling stability during the following 70 cycles (Figure  7b), which is compared with the recently reported CFC-based sulfur cathodes (Figure  7c).[30] The corresponding detailed values are listed in Table S2 in the Supporting Information. Although porous carbon fibers/vanadium nitride arrays/sulfur electrode obtains the higher areal capacity of 10.61 mAh $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at the sulfur loading of $8.1\ \mathrm{mg\cm^{-2}}$ , it needs excessive electrolyte $(\mathrm{E}/\mathrm{S}=20)$ to achieve such high sulfur utilization, which would decrease the energy density significantly, thus, not suitable for practical use. Therefore, considering both sulfur loading and electrolyte dose, the performance of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC electrode at the high sulfur loading is remarkable among those CFC-based sulfur cathodes. As shown in Table 1, we also compared the performance of Co, N-CNTsCNS/CFC host with other carbon hybrid sulfur cathodes,[31] demonstrating that its performance is still outstanding.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, by using Co-MOFs array grown on CFC as precursors, we synthesized a multiscale, hierarchical Co, N-doped carbonaceous composite (denoted as Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC), which is consist of free-standing 3D carbon fiber skeleton decorated with vertical 2D carbon nanosheets array rooted by interwoven 1D CNTs. Having large amount of active sites and fast electron/ions pathways, Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC can effectively anchor and convert polysulfides. The codoping of Co and N heteroatoms can strengthen the interaction between Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC and polysulfides and promote redox kinetics, which has been demonstrated by experiment and theoretical calculation. More importantly, the interwoven CNTs with Co, N-doping can induce spatially selective precipitation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , thus, change the growth of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ from conventional 2D to special 3D, which is beneficial for high sulfur utilization.
Hence, as a polysulfides host, Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC exhibits a high reversible capacity of 1185  mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~C~}$ , excellent long-term cycling stability (a low decay rate of $0.07\%$ per cycle for 250 cycles at $0.5~\mathrm{C}]$ , superior rate performance (779  mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $2\mathrm{~C,~1~C=1675~mA~g^{-1})}$ , much better than its counterparts (CFC and Co, N-CNS/CFC). Even at a high sulfur loading of $10.20~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ with a low $\mathrm{E/S}$ value of 6.94, Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC can still obtain a high reversible areal capacity 7.34 mAh $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with cycling stability at $0.1\mathrm{{C}}$ .
## 4. Experimental Section
Preparation of $C o$ -Based MOFs Array on Carbon Fiber Cloth (Denoted as Co-MOFs Array/CFC): First, $2.60\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{i}$ 2-MIM and $\ensuremath{\mathsf{1.17}}\ \ensuremath{\mathsf{g}}\ \ensuremath{\mathsf{C o}}$ $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ were completely dissolved in $80~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, respectively. Then, the above two solutions were mixed together under agitation. Second, a piece of clean CFC was immersed in the mixture solution for $4h$ under room temperature and taken out, cleaned with deionized water, and dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ overnight.
Preparation of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC, and Co, N-CNS/CFC: The as-prepared Co-MOFs array/CFC was annealed in ${\sf H}_{2}/{\sf A}{\sf r}$ (1:9 by volume) at $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ . During the heating process, the $\mathsf{A r/H}_{2}$ flow was allowed to bubble through pure DMF solution to provide DMF vapor as carbon source. After cooling, the product of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC was obtained. Co, N-CNS/CFC was obtained by the same process without DMF vapor.
Preparation of Blank Electrolyte and $L i_{2}S_{6}$ Catholyte: The blank electrolyte was synthesized by dissolving $\mathsf{1.0~M}$ LiTFSI into DOL/DME (1:1 by volume) with $1w t\%$ $\mathsf{L i N O}_{3}$ . The ${\mathsf{L i}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{6}$ catholyte was made by adding sublimed sulfur and $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ (5:1 by weight) into the above blank electrolyte and stirring in Ar filled glove box for ${\mathsf{12h}}$ .
Materials Characterization: The morphology and microstructure were measured by SEM (JEOL JSM-7001F) and TEM (JEOL JEM-2100F). The XRD patterns of the products were analyzed by a Bruker D8 Focus X-ray diffractometer equipped with a Ni-filtered $\mathsf{C u}~K_{\alpha}$ radiation $(\lambda=0.75406\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) source. Raman spectra were collected by LabRAM (HR800, Horiba Jobin-Yvon) with a $574\ n m$ Ar-ion laser. XPS tests were carried out by a Thermo Fisher Scientific instrument (ESCALAB 250Xi). The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller-specific surface areas were tested using a Quadrasorb SI-MP analyzer. Thermo gravimetric analysis was conducted by a STA7200RV apparatus (Hitachi High-Tech).
Electrochemical Measurements: The free-standing CFC, Co, N-CNS/ CFC, and Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC were cut into disks with diameters of $12\ m m$ and used as cathodes. $60~\upmu\up L$ of 0.2, 0.5, and 1 m ${\mathsf{L i}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}_{6}$ catholyte were dropped onto the cathodes, corresponding to 2.03, 5.10, and $10.20~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ of sulfur loading, respectively. The areal mass of Co, N-CNTs-CNS/CFC is about $73.64~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . So when the sulfur loading is $10.20~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , the weight ratio of the host material to sulfur is about 4:3, which is the lowest among the recently related works (Table S3, Supporting Information). Then the cathodes were covered by Celgard 2400 films, followed by 20  uL of the blank electrolyte. Finally, the lithium metal anodes were put on the top of the Celgard 2400 films and assembled into CR2032 cells. The whole assemble processes were carried in the Ar-filled glove box $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}/\mathsf{O}_{2}<0.7$ ppm). The galvanostatic charge–discharge measurements were carried out on a Neware battery testing system. The cyclic voltammetry and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy were tested by an Autolab (PGSTAT302N) electrochemical workstation. In this work, the specific capacity was calculated according to the weight of S.
Details of First-Principles Calculations: These calculations were performed via the plane-wave based DFT method as implemented in the Vienna ab initio simulation package.[32] For the core-valence electron interaction, ultrasoft pseudopotentials[33] are used, and the generalized gradient approximation in the Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof parametrization is adopted for the exchange-correlation functional.[34] To account the dispersion interaction, the van der Waals density functional is invoked as a nonlocal correlation functional in all the calculations.[35] A cutoff energy of $500~\mathrm{eV}$ for planewave basis set was adopted and the convergence criterion for the residual force and energy was set to 0.01  eV $\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-\overline{{1}}}$ and ${\sf10}^{-6}{\sf e}{\sf V},$ respectively, during the structure relaxation.
Supercells consisting of $4\times4$ graphene and Co (111) unit cells are considered in the present calculations. A vacuum height of $\rceil5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ in the $z$ -direction was adopted to avoid the interaction between two periodically repeated units. The Brillouin zone was represented by a Monkhorst– Pack special $k$ -point mesh of size $\mathsf{l}\times\mathsf{l}\times\mathsf{l}$ and $4\times4\times1$ for geometry optimizations and band structure computations. Binding energy of hybrid structure is calculated via: $E_{\mathsf{B}}=E_{\mathsf{h y b}}-(E_{\mathsf{s u b}}+E_{\mathsf{L i254}}),\mathsf{\Omega}^{[36]}$ where $\bar{E}_{\mathrm{hyb}}$ and $E_{\mathsf{L i}2\mathsf{S}4}$ are energy of the hybrid system, and isolate $\mathsf{L i}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}$ molecule from the calculations, $E_{\mathsf{s u b}}$ indicates the energy of Graphene, Graphitic N, Pyridinic N, Co@Graphene, $\mathsf{C o@}$ Graphitic N, $\mathsf{C o@}$ Pyridinic N. To explore the charge transfer between different parts in the hybrid system, the bader’s analysis method is adopted in the present work.
